            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-mult-ind">
            <title>Multiplication Rule for Independent Events</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-fung-inf">
            <title>Fungal Infections in Wildlife</title>
            <p>
                A wildlife biologist studies fungal infections in two separate animal
                populations. Of the 60 bats tested, 9 have a fungal infection. Of the
                70 frogs tested, 14 have a fungal infection. The biologist randomly
                selects one bat and one frog.
            </p>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li>
                    What is the probability that both selected animals have fungal infections?
                </li>
                <li>
                    What is the probability that neither selected animal has a fungal infection?
                </li>
                <li>
                    What is the probability that only the selected frog has a fungal infection?
                </li>
                <li>
                    What is the probability that only the selected bat has a fungal infection?
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
        <title>Conditional Probability</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-dep-events">
            <title>Dependent Events</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-cond-prob">
            <title>Conditional Probability</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-blood-inf">
            <title>Blood Infusion Neccesity</title>
            <p>
                A study was conducted to ascertain factors that influence a physician's decision to transfuse a patient. A sample of 49 attending physicians was selected. Each physician was asked a question concerning the frequency with which an unnecessary transfusion was given because anout physician suggested it. The same question was asked of a smaple of 71 residents.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-blood-inf">
                <title>Data used for Physician Type and Blood Infusion Association</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                    top="minor"
                    bottom="minor"
                    left="minor"
                    right="minor"
                    halign="center">

                    <row header="yes">
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell colspan="5">Frequency of Unnecessary Transfusion</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell>Physician Type</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>Very Frequent</line>
                        <line>(1 per week)</line>
                    </cell>
                        <cell>
                        <line>Frequent</line>
                        <line>(1 per 2 weeks)</line>
                    </cell>
                        <cell>
                        <line>Occasionally</line>
                        <line>(1 per month)</line>
                    </cell>
                        <cell>
                        <line>Rarely</line>
                        <line>(1 per 2 months)</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>Never</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                    <cell>Attending</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>31</cell>
                    <cell>13</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                    <cell>Resident</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>13</cell>
                    <cell>28</cell>
                    <cell>23</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <ol>
                <li>
                What is the probability that a randomly selected physician is an attending
                physician, given that the physician answered <q>Rarely</q>? Round your answer to three decimal places.
                </li>

                <li>
                Find the proportion of physicians who answered <q>Rarely</q> who are resident physicians. Include probability notation in your answer and round to three decimal places.
                </li>

                <li>
                Thirteen attending physicians answered <q>Never</q>, and thirteen
                resident physicians answered <q>Frequent</q>. Does this mean that
                the probability that an attending physician answered
                <q>Never</q> is equal to the probability that a resident physician
                answered <q>Frequent</q>? Calculate both probabilities and explain.
                </li>

                <li>
                Find the probability that a randomly selected attending physician
                answered <q>Rarely</q>. Compare this with the probability found in
                the first question. Explain why the probabilities are different
                even though both calculations use the same table entry.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </example>

