    <subsection>
        <title>Examples</title>
        <example xml:id="ex-anti-res">
            <title>Antibiotic-Resistant Cultures</title>

            <p>
                A microbiology laboratory has 18 bacterial cultures in identical
                tubes. Six contain bacteria resistant to an antibiotic, and 12
                contain bacteria susceptible to it. A technician randomly selects
                two tubes.

                <ol>
                <li>
                    Suppose the technician records the first tube's result and
                    returns it to the rack before selecting the second tube. Find
                    the probability that both selected cultures are resistant.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Suppose the technician sets the first tube aside before
                    selecting the second tube. Find the probability that both
                    selected cultures are resistant.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Explain why the probabilities with replacement and without
                    replacement are different.
                </li>

                <li>
                    In which sampling method are the two selections independent?
                    Explain.
                </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            </example>

            <example xml:id="ex-nest-loc-fled">
            <title>Nest Location and Fledging Success</title>

            <p>
                Ecologists monitored 180 songbird nests. They recorded whether each
                nest was located in a natural tree cavity or an artificial nest box
                and whether the nest successfully produced at least one fledgling.
            </p>
            
            <table xml:id="table-nest-loc-fled">
                <title>Songbird Nest Location and Fledging Success</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes"
                        top="minor"
                        bottom="minor"
                        left="minor"
                        right="minor"
                        halign="center">

                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell>Nest Location</cell>
                    <cell>Successful</cell>
                    <cell>Unsuccessful</cell>
                    <cell>Total</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                    <cell>Natural Tree Cavity</cell>
                    <cell>54</cell>
                    <cell>18</cell>
                    <cell>72</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                    <cell>Artificial Nest Box</cell>
                    <cell>72</cell>
                    <cell>36</cell>
                    <cell>108</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row top="medium">
                    <cell>Total</cell>
                    <cell>126</cell>
                    <cell>54</cell>
                    <cell>180</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                <ol>
                <li>
                    Find the probability that a randomly selected nest was located
                    in an artificial nest box and was successful.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Among nests located in artificial nest boxes, find the
                    probability that a randomly selected nest was successful.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Among successful nests, find the probability that a randomly
                    selected nest was located in an artificial nest box.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The same table entry is used in all three calculations. Explain
                    why the resulting probabilities are different.
                </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            </example>

            <example xml:id="ex-sea-turtles">
            <title>Plastic Ingestion in Sea Turtles</title>

            <p>
                At a wildlife rehabilitation center, <m>60\%</m> of admitted sea
                turtles are juveniles and <m>40\%</m> are adults. Evidence of plastic
                ingestion is found in <m>30\%</m> of the juvenile turtles and
                <m>15\%</m> of the adult turtles.

                <ol>
                <li>
                    Find the probability that a randomly selected admitted turtle
                    is a juvenile with evidence of plastic ingestion.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Find the probability that a randomly selected admitted turtle
                    is an adult with evidence of plastic ingestion.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Find the probability that a randomly selected admitted turtle
                    has evidence of plastic ingestion.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Given that an admitted turtle has evidence of plastic ingestion,
                    find the probability that it is a juvenile.
                </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            </example>
    </subsection>
</section>